<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mwanga II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1868">1868</a>–<a href="/years/1903">1903</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Mwanga II of Buganda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mwanga II was kabaka (king) of Buganda from 1884, succeeding Mutesa I. He ordered the killing of Christian converts at his court in 1885–1887, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uganda-martyrs">Uganda Martyrs</a>, and was deposed and restored amid civil war between Muslim, Catholic and Protestant factions. After rebelling against British rule in 1897 he was captured and exiled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seychelles">Seychelles</a>, where he died.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mwanga-ii.jpg" alt="Mwanga II" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Deutsche Kolonialgesellschaft (DKG) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Mwanga_II_Buganda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
